1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,426
Picture an oni, a demon
from Japanese folklore.

2
00:00:05,726 --> 00:00:09,513
Red or blue skin, horns
on its head, a loincloth

3
00:00:09,513 --> 00:00:12,977
of tiger skin, and an iron
club in its hand.

4
00:00:13,277 --> 00:00:19,303
That is how oni appear today, in picture
books, at festivals, and every February

5
00:00:19,303 --> 00:00:25,177
at Setsubun, when families throw roasted
beans to drive them out of the house.

6
00:00:25,477 --> 00:00:28,615
But the oldest Japanese
records are still here.

7
00:00:28,915 --> 00:00:31,346
So, why do oni wear tiger skin?

8
00:00:31,646 --> 00:00:33,246
And did they always?

9
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:38,869
In this episode, we check four
things that are often said about oni.

10
00:00:39,169 --> 00:00:41,367
That oni always looked like this.

11
00:00:41,667 --> 00:00:43,845
That the word oni means hidden.

12
00:00:44,145 --> 00:00:48,909
That the horns and the tiger skin
come from a direction on the compass.

13
00:00:49,209 --> 00:00:52,377
And that oni are the ones
who get chased away.

14
00:00:52,677 --> 00:00:57,133
One by one, we will hold each
of them up against the old sources.

15
00:01:02,067 --> 00:01:08,531
Japan's oldest official history, the Nihon
Shoki, was completed in seven twenty.

16
00:01:08,831 --> 00:01:11,359
It is written in Chinese characters,

17
00:01:11,359 --> 00:01:14,941
and it uses the character
that is read as oni today.

18
00:01:15,241 --> 00:01:20,326
In early Chinese writing, that character
was tied to the spirits of the dead.

19
00:01:21,233 --> 00:01:24,265
The chronicle tells
of the year six sixty-one.

20
00:01:24,565 --> 00:01:28,071
Empress Saimei had moved
her court to a temporary

21
00:01:28,071 --> 00:01:30,920
palace in Kyushu, in the west of Japan.

22
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:33,343
That summer, she died there.

23
00:01:33,643 --> 00:01:38,755
Then, after the crown prince had
brought her remains back to the Palace

24
00:01:38,755 --> 00:01:43,795
of Ihase, that evening, on the top
of Mount Asakura, there was an oni.

25
00:01:44,095 --> 00:01:47,680
It wore a large hat, and it
looked down on the funeral.

26
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:50,809
Everyone who saw it cried out in wonder.

27
00:01:51,109 --> 00:01:56,472
When W. G. Aston translated this
passage into English in eighteen

28
00:01:56,472 --> 00:02:01,081
ninety-six, he wrote demon,
and added a note: or spirit.

29
00:02:01,381 --> 00:02:06,084
The chronicle says nothing about
horns, tiger skin, or a club.

30
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,509
About seventy years after these
events, in seven thirty-three,

31
00:02:11,509 --> 00:02:16,683
the province of Izumo completed
a record of its land, the Izumo Fudoki.

32
00:02:16,983 --> 00:02:21,144
It explains the name of one
village, Ayo, with a story.

33
00:02:21,444 --> 00:02:24,763
Long ago, a man was farming
a mountain field.

34
00:02:25,063 --> 00:02:27,764
A one-eyed oni came and ate his son.

35
00:02:28,064 --> 00:02:32,361
The parents hid in a bamboo thicket,
and the bamboo leaves stirred.

36
00:02:32,661 --> 00:02:36,501
As he was being eaten,
the son cried out, ayo, ayo.

37
00:02:36,801 --> 00:02:38,979
And so the place was called Ayo.

38
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:42,112
So the first claim does not hold.

39
00:02:42,412 --> 00:02:48,093
In two of the oldest records, one oni
wears a hat and watches in silence.

40
00:02:48,393 --> 00:02:50,506
The other has a single eye.

41
00:02:50,806 --> 00:02:54,163
Neither record mentions
horns or tiger skin.

42
00:02:55,067 --> 00:02:58,083
The second claim is about the word itself.

43
00:02:58,383 --> 00:03:01,756
In the nine thirties,
the scholar Minamoto no

44
00:03:01,756 --> 00:03:05,590
Shitagou compiled a dictionary,
the Wamyō Ruijushō.

45
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:10,375
Under the character for oni, he
gives the Japanese reading, oni.

46
00:03:10,675 --> 00:03:12,403
Then he adds a note.

47
00:03:12,703 --> 00:03:17,348
Some say that it comes from on,
the sound of the character for hidden,

48
00:03:17,348 --> 00:03:21,521
because oni hide themselves and do
not wish to show their form.

49
00:03:21,821 --> 00:03:27,046
The entry ends with a short definition:
the soul of a person who has died.

50
00:03:27,967 --> 00:03:31,994
So the idea is very old,
more than a thousand years.

51
00:03:32,294 --> 00:03:35,387
But even then, it came
with the words some say.

52
00:03:35,687 --> 00:03:39,037
It is an early guess, not a proven origin.

53
00:03:39,337 --> 00:03:44,678
In the Tales of Ise, a collection
that took shape around the tenth century,

54
00:03:44,678 --> 00:03:50,597
a woman is carried off on a stormy night,
and an oni swallows her in a single gulp.

55
00:03:50,897 --> 00:03:53,607
The tale never describes the oni.

56
00:03:53,907 --> 00:03:57,760
And a note at the end
of the episode explains that the oni

57
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,681
was really her own brothers,
who had come to take her back.

58
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,193
Faces began to appear in pictures.

59
00:04:05,493 --> 00:04:09,692
In the Illustrated Legends
of the Kitano Tenjin Shrine,

60
00:04:09,692 --> 00:04:13,347
a scroll painted in the late
thirteenth century,

61
00:04:13,347 --> 00:04:17,779
strange figures with horns
and wild hair gather by a gate.

62
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:24,379
Over the following centuries, artists kept
giving oni horns, fangs and fierce faces.

63
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:29,235
The third claim explains
the horns and the tiger skin.

64
00:04:29,535 --> 00:04:32,339
In seventeen seventy-nine, the artist

65
00:04:32,339 --> 00:04:35,674
Toriyama Sekien published
a book of monsters.

66
00:04:35,974 --> 00:04:38,801
Beside his oni, he wrote a short note.

67
00:04:39,101 --> 00:04:44,786
People call the northeast, the direction
of the ox and the tiger, the demon gate.

68
00:04:45,086 --> 00:04:48,889
When oni are painted today,
they wear the horns of an ox

69
00:04:48,889 --> 00:04:52,901
on their heads and the skin
of a tiger around their waists.

70
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:57,663
This shape, he says, is said to join
the ox and the tiger together.

71
00:04:58,567 --> 00:05:01,288
It is a neat explanation, and Sekien

72
00:05:01,288 --> 00:05:04,539
himself presents it
as something people say.

73
00:05:04,839 --> 00:05:09,624
He wrote it down more than a thousand
years after the oldest records,

74
00:05:09,624 --> 00:05:14,831
and about five hundred years after
the horned figures in the Kitano scroll.

75
00:05:15,131 --> 00:05:20,535
Whether the picture or the explanation
came first, the sources do not tell us.

76
00:05:21,467 --> 00:05:24,349
The last claim brings us back to Setsubun.

77
00:05:24,649 --> 00:05:28,058
A court rite to drive
away disease is recorded

78
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:31,695
in seven oh six, in a year
when many people died.

79
00:05:31,995 --> 00:05:36,113
In a later court manual,
the Gōke Shidai, from about nine

80
00:05:36,113 --> 00:05:40,598
hundred years ago, an official
called the hōsō leads the rite.

81
00:05:40,898 --> 00:05:44,721
He shouts, strikes his shield
three times with his spear,

82
00:05:44,721 --> 00:05:47,657
and the courtiers join
in the cry and chase.

83
00:05:47,957 --> 00:05:53,173
Then the manual adds one more line:
the courtiers shoot arrows at the hōsō.

84
00:05:53,473 --> 00:05:55,086
Beans came later.

85
00:05:55,386 --> 00:06:00,166
In fourteen forty-seven, a Zen
monk wrote down the cry, oni out,

86
00:06:00,166 --> 00:06:05,400
fortune in, and called it the way
demons were driven out in this land.

87
00:06:06,333 --> 00:06:08,075
So the roles changed.

88
00:06:08,375 --> 00:06:13,441
In that court manual, the figure who
led the chase also became a target.

89
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:17,591
And by the fifteenth century,
beans were being thrown,

90
00:06:17,591 --> 00:06:20,279
and the oni was the one sent outside.

91
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,631
So, why do oni wear tiger skin?

92
00:06:23,931 --> 00:06:29,263
One explanation links it to the ox
and the tiger, the unlucky northeast.

93
00:06:29,563 --> 00:06:34,918
Sekien recorded it in seventeen
seventy-nine, long after the first oni.

94
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:40,664
The oldest records show something else:
a figure in a large hat on a mountain,

95
00:06:40,664 --> 00:06:45,757
a one-eyed creature in a mountain
field, and a word that may mean hidden.

96
00:06:46,057 --> 00:06:50,651
The horns, the tiger skin and the club
came later, through pictures.

97
00:06:51,567 --> 00:06:53,894
Every story has an origin.

98
00:06:54,194 --> 00:06:57,925
Sometimes, it begins
with something no one could see.

99
00:06:58,833 --> 00:07:04,751
This video is based on historical texts
and public museum and library collections.

100
00:07:05,051 --> 00:07:07,771
The narration uses synthetic speech.

101
00:07:08,071 --> 00:07:12,017
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

102
00:07:12,317 --> 00:07:15,085
If you enjoyed this story, please like

103
00:07:15,085 --> 00:07:18,437
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

104
00:07:18,737 --> 00:07:20,510
Thank you for watching.
